WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.624
Lesson 2 improves atomic reasoning by forcing one decision before all the others.

2
00:00:05.624 --> 00:00:11.247
If two nuclei have the same proton number, they belong to the same element family.

3
00:00:11.247 --> 00:00:16.871
Only after that should the learner compare mass number and neutron count.

4
00:00:16.871 --> 00:00:22.495
That is the heart of isotope thinking.

5
00:00:22.495 --> 00:00:28.118
Isotopes are the same element with different neutron counts, and their stability can differ without their identity changing.

6
00:00:28.118 --> 00:00:33.742
Keeping those ideas separate prevents students from mistaking a heavier isotope for a different element.

7
00:00:33.742 --> 00:00:39.365
Keep this takeaway in view: Isotopes are easiest when identity, neutron difference, and stability are kept apart.

8
00:00:39.365 --> 00:00:44.989
Identity first means same proton count.

9
00:00:44.989 --> 00:00:50.613
Proton number decides whether two nuclei belong to the same element.

10
00:00:50.613 --> 00:00:56.236
Isotope difference means different neutrons.

11
00:00:56.236 --> 00:01:01.860
Neutron number changes the isotope while keeping the element family fixed.

12
00:01:01.860 --> 00:01:09.786
This diagram matters because it trains the learner to compare nuclei in the right order.

13
00:01:09.786 --> 00:01:17.711
The same badge count means the same proton number, and that settles the identity question immediately.

14
00:01:17.711 --> 00:01:25.637
Only then does the different total mass number become meaningful, because it tells us the neutron number must differ.

15
00:01:25.637 --> 00:01:33.563
The diagram also leaves space for the stability question, which is related to the neutron-proton balance but should not be confused with the element identity itself.

16
00:01:33.563 --> 00:01:41.489
Keep this takeaway in view: The diagram keeps identity and isotope difference in the right order.

17
00:01:41.489 --> 00:01:49.414
Shared badge: Equal proton count means the nuclei belong to the same element family.

18
00:01:49.414 --> 00:01:57.340
Different total: A different mass number means the neutron count must be different.

19
00:01:57.340 --> 00:02:03.951
A family-name analogy helps because it keeps sameness and difference working together.

20
00:02:03.951 --> 00:02:10.562
Two siblings can share the same family name, which is like sharing the same proton number and therefore the same element identity.

21
00:02:10.562 --> 00:02:17.173
But they can carry different backpack loads, which is like having different neutron counts and mass numbers.

22
00:02:17.173 --> 00:02:23.784
Those load differences can affect how balanced or stable each sibling feels, but they do not rewrite the family identity.

23
00:02:23.784 --> 00:02:30.395
The analogy helps the learner trust that isotopes are same-element variants, not separate elements.

24
00:02:30.395 --> 00:02:37.006
Keep this takeaway in view: The analogy makes isotope comparison feel like same-family identity plus different nuclear load.

25
00:02:37.006 --> 00:02:43.617
Family name means like proton count.

26
00:02:43.617 --> 00:02:50.228
The shared identity comes first and does not change when the extra load changes.

27
00:02:50.228 --> 00:02:56.839
Backpack load means like neutron number.

28
00:02:56.839 --> 00:03:03.450
The extra load changes the variant without changing the family.

29
00:03:03.450 --> 00:03:08.550
The central relation for this lesson is that neutron number equals mass number minus proton number.

30
00:03:08.550 --> 00:03:13.650
But that subtraction only matters once the learner has already recognized that the proton number fixes the element identity.

31
00:03:13.650 --> 00:03:18.750
The formula therefore serves a comparison story.

32
00:03:18.750 --> 00:03:23.850
First confirm same proton number.

33
00:03:23.850 --> 00:03:28.950
Then calculate the neutron numbers.

34
00:03:28.950 --> 00:03:34.050
Then, if the lesson asks, talk about how stability may differ between those isotopes.

35
00:03:34.050 --> 00:03:39.150
This order keeps the topic clean.

36
00:03:39.150 --> 00:03:44.250
Keep this takeaway in view: The formula works best when identity is settled before subtraction begins.

37
00:03:44.250 --> 00:03:49.350
If Z matches: The element identity is already fixed before the mass comparison begins.

38
00:03:49.350 --> 00:03:54.450
If A differs: The neutron count must differ, which is why the nuclei are isotopes.

39
00:03:54.450 --> 00:03:59.550
same proton number => same element.

40
00:03:59.550 --> 00:04:04.650
neutron number = mass number - proton number.

41
00:04:04.650 --> 00:04:09.750
Do not let a different mass number trick you into declaring a different element before checking proton count.

42
00:04:09.750 --> 00:04:15.448
A dependable isotope routine has three moves.

43
00:04:15.448 --> 00:04:21.146
First compare proton number and decide whether the nuclei are the same element.

44
00:04:21.146 --> 00:04:26.844
Second calculate neutron number using mass number minus proton number.

45
00:04:26.844 --> 00:04:32.542
Third discuss any stability implication only after the identity and neutron differences are clear.

46
00:04:32.542 --> 00:04:38.240
This pattern matters because it stops learners from using mass number as the first identity test.

47
00:04:38.240 --> 00:04:43.938
Keep this takeaway in view: Identity first, neutrons second, stability third is the safest isotope routine.

48
00:04:43.938 --> 00:04:49.636
Step 1 means check proton number.

49
00:04:49.636 --> 00:04:55.334
Identity comes from Z, not from the mass number.

50
00:04:55.334 --> 00:05:01.032
Step 2 means find the neutrons.

51
00:05:01.032 --> 00:05:06.730
Use A minus Z to compute the neutron count for each isotope.

52
00:05:06.730 --> 00:05:11.528
If the learner checks mass number first, two isotopes can look like two different substances immediately.

53
00:05:11.528 --> 00:05:16.326
That is backwards.

54
00:05:16.326 --> 00:05:21.124
The first question is always proton number, because proton number defines the element.

55
00:05:21.124 --> 00:05:25.922
Only after that does a different mass number tell us the nuclei are different isotopes of that same element.

56
00:05:25.922 --> 00:05:30.720
Giving this point its own scene strengthens the whole topic.

57
00:05:30.720 --> 00:05:35.518
Keep this takeaway in view: Proton number should always win the first decision in isotope work.

58
00:05:35.518 --> 00:05:40.316
Wrong opening means mass first.

59
00:05:40.316 --> 00:05:45.114
Starting with mass number can tempt the learner into a false identity change.

60
00:05:45.114 --> 00:05:49.912
Right opening means protons first.

61
00:05:49.912 --> 00:05:54.710
Proton number settles the element before any neutron difference is discussed.

62
00:05:54.710 --> 00:06:00.862
Two isotopes can belong to the same element and still behave differently in terms of stability.

63
00:06:00.862 --> 00:06:07.014
That is why stability must be treated as a separate question.

64
00:06:07.014 --> 00:06:13.167
The proton count fixes identity, the neutron count identifies the isotope difference, and the nuclear balance influences stability.

65
00:06:13.167 --> 00:06:19.319
If all three are blended into one sentence, students lose the logic that the lesson is trying to build.

66
00:06:19.319 --> 00:06:25.471
Keep this takeaway in view: Stability is an extra comparison layer, not the definition of isotope identity.

67
00:06:25.471 --> 00:06:31.623
Same element means identity fixed.

68
00:06:31.623 --> 00:06:37.776
Matching proton count keeps the nuclei in the same element family.

69
00:06:37.776 --> 00:06:43.928
Different isotope means neutrons differ.

70
00:06:43.928 --> 00:06:50.080
A changed neutron count creates a different isotope of that element.

71
00:06:50.080 --> 00:06:53.175
Ask four checks.

72
00:06:53.175 --> 00:06:56.269
Do same proton numbers mean the same element?

73
00:06:56.269 --> 00:06:59.364
Yes.

74
00:06:59.364 --> 00:07:02.459
Do different mass numbers imply different neutron counts?

75
00:07:02.459 --> 00:07:05.553
Yes.

76
00:07:05.553 --> 00:07:08.648
Can two isotopes of the same element have different stability?

77
00:07:08.648 --> 00:07:11.743
Yes.

78
00:07:11.743 --> 00:07:14.837
And should mass number be checked before proton number when deciding identity?

79
00:07:14.837 --> 00:07:17.932
No.

80
00:07:17.932 --> 00:07:21.027
Those checks show the isotope logic is now in the right order.

81
00:07:21.027 --> 00:07:24.121
Keep this takeaway in view: The quick check confirms that the isotope lesson is being read in the right order.

82
00:07:24.121 --> 00:07:27.216
Identity check means same protons.

83
00:07:27.216 --> 00:07:30.311
Equal proton count means the same element family.

84
00:07:30.311 --> 00:07:33.405
Isotope check means different neutrons.

85
00:07:33.405 --> 00:07:36.500
The isotope difference appears because the neutron count changes.

86
00:07:36.500 --> 00:07:41.232
The worked example that follows asks whether two nuclei are isotopes of the same element and how their neutron counts compare.

87
00:07:41.232 --> 00:07:45.964
Start by checking proton number.

88
00:07:45.964 --> 00:07:50.695
Then subtract to find each neutron count.

89
00:07:50.695 --> 00:07:55.427
Then explain why same proton number plus different neutron counts means same element, different isotope.

90
00:07:55.427 --> 00:08:00.159
That route makes the answer feel structured instead of improvised.

91
00:08:00.159 --> 00:08:04.891
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is easiest when proton number settles identity before the neutron comparison begins.

92
00:08:04.891 --> 00:08:09.623
First move: Check whether the proton numbers match.

93
00:08:09.623 --> 00:08:14.355
Second move: Calculate each neutron number.

94
00:08:14.355 --> 00:08:19.086
same Z => same element.

95
00:08:19.086 --> 00:08:23.818
neutrons = A - Z.

96
00:08:23.818 --> 00:08:28.550
Do not let the larger mass number speak before the proton number has spoken.
